WEBVTT

00:00:00.342 --> 00:00:03.038
Last time, the water escaped as steam, and

00:00:03.058 --> 00:00:03.823
we just let it go.

00:00:04.485 --> 00:00:06.250
To keep it, we have to catch that

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.296
vapour and cool it back into a liquid.

00:00:08.878 --> 00:00:11.194
That's called distillation, and it works well for

00:00:11.236 --> 00:00:11.759
salt water.

00:00:12.441 --> 00:00:14.665
But with two liquids, like ethanol and water,

00:00:15.147 --> 00:00:17.511
a simple setup does a poor job, and

00:00:17.551 --> 00:00:19.715
adding a tall column packed with glass beads

00:00:20.036 --> 00:00:21.139
does a much better one.

00:00:22.043 --> 00:00:25.158
So why does a fractionating column improve liquid

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:25.761
separation?

00:00:26.769 --> 00:00:28.034
Here's simple distillation.

00:00:28.739 --> 00:00:30.267
Heat salt water in a flask.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.439
Water molecules escape as vapour, which just means

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.466
a substance in its gas state, but the

00:00:35.548 --> 00:00:36.651
salt ions don't.

00:00:37.194 --> 00:00:39.759
Salt is non-volatile, so it doesn't evaporate at

00:00:39.801 --> 00:00:40.381
these temperatures.

00:00:41.284 --> 00:00:43.769
The vapour flows into the condenser, a tube

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.935
with cold water running through an outer jacket.

00:00:46.637 --> 00:00:48.766
The cold glass takes energy from the vapour,

00:00:49.089 --> 00:00:51.238
so it condenses back into liquid water.

00:00:52.002 --> 00:00:54.353
That liquid is called the distillate, and it

00:00:54.414 --> 00:00:55.559
drips into the receiver.

00:00:56.244 --> 00:00:57.798
The salt stays behind in the flask.

00:00:58.953 --> 00:01:00.062
Two details matter.

00:01:00.689 --> 00:01:02.853
The thermometer bulb sits level with the side

00:01:02.915 --> 00:01:04.838
arm, so it measures the vapour that's about

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:06.683
to leave, not the boiling liquid.

00:01:07.307 --> 00:01:09.442
For pure water, it reads a steady hundred

00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:09.804
degrees.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.698
And the apparatus must never be sealed.

00:01:13.201 --> 00:01:15.362
The receiver stays open to the air, because

00:01:15.443 --> 00:01:17.885
heating a closed system builds up pressure, and

00:01:17.906 --> 00:01:18.725
the glass could burst.

00:01:19.796 --> 00:01:21.142
Now try two liquids.

00:01:21.846 --> 00:01:24.752
Ethanol boils at seventy eight degrees, and water

00:01:24.773 --> 00:01:25.694
boils at a hundred.

00:01:26.337 --> 00:01:28.245
You might expect the ethanol to boil off

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:30.275
at seventy eight, and leave the water behind.

00:01:31.141 --> 00:01:32.025
That's not what happens.

00:01:32.811 --> 00:01:35.417
A mixture that's seventeen percent ethanol by mass

00:01:35.619 --> 00:01:38.005
starts boiling at about eighty nine degrees,

00:01:38.528 --> 00:01:40.172
and both kinds of molecule escape.

00:01:40.955 --> 00:01:43.300
Ethanol molecules are held less strongly by their

00:01:43.341 --> 00:01:45.224
neighbours, so more of them get away.

00:01:45.968 --> 00:01:48.517
The vapour is richer in ethanol, about sixty

00:01:48.558 --> 00:01:50.646
two percent, but that's still a lot of

00:01:50.707 --> 00:01:50.947
water.

00:01:51.769 --> 00:01:52.774
So what if we do it again?

00:01:53.459 --> 00:01:55.716
Condense that vapour, and boil the new liquid.

00:01:56.422 --> 00:01:58.671
Now the vapour is about eighty percent ethanol.

00:01:59.275 --> 00:02:01.164
Do it again, and it's about eighty six,

00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:02.672
then about eighty nine.

00:02:03.476 --> 00:02:06.478
Each cycle of boiling and condensing enriches the

00:02:06.499 --> 00:02:08.780
vapour, which means it raises the share of

00:02:08.821 --> 00:02:11.162
the more volatile liquid, the one that evaporates

00:02:11.203 --> 00:02:11.682
more easily.

00:02:12.526 --> 00:02:13.922
Notice the steps are getting smaller.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:16.844
Simple distillation gives you only one cycle.

00:02:17.328 --> 00:02:19.606
To separate two liquids well, you need many.

00:02:20.637 --> 00:02:23.312
That's what a fractionating column does, automatically.

00:02:23.976 --> 00:02:26.342
It's a tall tube packed with glass beads,

00:02:26.543 --> 00:02:28.146
between the flask and the condenser.

00:02:28.809 --> 00:02:31.037
It's hottest at the bottom and cooler towards

00:02:31.078 --> 00:02:31.378
the top.

00:02:32.282 --> 00:02:34.848
Rising vapour condenses on the cooler beads, and

00:02:34.889 --> 00:02:36.112
that liquid trickles down.

00:02:36.895 --> 00:02:39.059
Hotter vapour rising from below boils some of

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.285
it again, and more of the ethanol escapes.

00:02:42.048 --> 00:02:44.274
This happens over and over up the column,

00:02:44.737 --> 00:02:46.823
so each level holds vapour a little richer

00:02:46.865 --> 00:02:48.188
in ethanol than the level below.

00:02:48.992 --> 00:02:51.323
By the top, the vapour is mostly ethanol,

00:02:51.666 --> 00:02:52.810
at about seventy eight degrees.

00:02:53.655 --> 00:02:55.618
The beads matter because they give the vapour

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.405
a huge surface to condense on, so lots

00:02:58.426 --> 00:03:00.229
of these little cycles happen in a short

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:00.510
height.

00:03:01.293 --> 00:03:03.839
A longer column gives even more cycles, so

00:03:03.901 --> 00:03:04.963
the separation gets better.

00:03:05.847 --> 00:03:08.415
You collect the distillate in parts, called fractions.

00:03:09.138 --> 00:03:11.485
While the thermometer stays near seventy eight, the

00:03:11.506 --> 00:03:13.091
fraction is mostly ethanol.

00:03:13.613 --> 00:03:15.694
When the reading climbs towards a hundred, the

00:03:15.755 --> 00:03:17.636
ethanol is running out, so you swap in

00:03:17.677 --> 00:03:18.357
a new receiver.

00:03:19.425 --> 00:03:20.556
But here's the limit.

00:03:21.141 --> 00:03:23.810
However tall the column, the distillate never gets

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.480
past about ninety six percent ethanol by mass.

00:03:27.383 --> 00:03:29.987
At that composition, the vapour has exactly the

00:03:30.027 --> 00:03:32.872
same composition as the liquid, so another cycle

00:03:32.932 --> 00:03:33.633
changes nothing.

00:03:34.394 --> 00:03:36.859
A mixture like this, which boils without changing

00:03:36.899 --> 00:03:39.223
its composition, is called an azeotrope.

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:42.371
It boils at one steady temperature, just like

00:03:42.392 --> 00:03:43.213
a pure substance.

00:03:43.876 --> 00:03:46.181
So a steady thermometer reading is a hint,

00:03:46.763 --> 00:03:47.885
not proof of purity.

00:03:48.889 --> 00:03:50.355
So which should you choose?

00:03:51.078 --> 00:03:53.401
If one part doesn't evaporate at all, like

00:03:53.502 --> 00:03:54.202
salt in water,

00:03:54.704 --> 00:03:56.406
or the boiling points are far apart,

00:03:57.028 --> 00:03:58.029
one cycle is enough.

00:03:58.713 --> 00:03:59.998
That's simple distillation.

00:04:00.662 --> 00:04:02.609
If two liquids mix and their boiling points

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:03.151
are close,

00:04:03.674 --> 00:04:05.039
use fractional distillation.

00:04:05.682 --> 00:04:08.007
You'll sometimes hear twenty five degrees apart as

00:04:08.028 --> 00:04:09.771
a rough guide, but it isn't a law.

00:04:10.474 --> 00:04:12.882
What matters is how much more easily one

00:04:12.943 --> 00:04:15.351
liquid evaporates, and how pure you need it.

00:04:16.276 --> 00:04:18.663
Oil refineries use giant columns like this to

00:04:18.704 --> 00:04:21.653
split crude oil into fractions, like petrol and

00:04:21.674 --> 00:04:21.954
diesel.

00:04:22.887 --> 00:04:25.849
Ethanol catches fire easily, so it's heated with

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.312
an electric heating mantle or a water bath,

00:04:28.633 --> 00:04:31.335
never a flame, with the apparatus clamped and

00:04:31.356 --> 00:04:32.516
the receiver open.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.725
Here's a way to remember the column.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.909
Simple distillation is one step up the ladder.

00:04:38.433 --> 00:04:40.177
A column climbs many steps,

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.905
and the ladder ends at the azeotrope.

00:04:45.646 --> 00:04:46.054
Your turn.

00:04:46.821 --> 00:04:49.705
A solution contains water and a dissolved blue

00:04:49.766 --> 00:04:51.688
dye that doesn't evaporate when heated.

00:04:52.389 --> 00:04:54.636
Name and explain a technique that could collect

00:04:54.676 --> 00:04:55.118
the water,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.565
and explain why filtration can't do it.

00:04:58.389 --> 00:04:59.276
Pause here to answer.

00:05:10.223 --> 00:05:11.490
Use simple distillation.

00:05:12.131 --> 00:05:15.054
Heat the solution, and the water evaporates while

00:05:15.074 --> 00:05:17.636
the non-volatile dye stays in the flask.

00:05:18.297 --> 00:05:21.186
The vapour condenses in the condenser and collects

00:05:21.347 --> 00:05:22.230
as pure water.

00:05:22.933 --> 00:05:25.950
Filtration can't work, because the dye is dissolved.

00:05:26.514 --> 00:05:28.624
Its particles are far smaller than the pores,

00:05:29.027 --> 00:05:30.796
so they pass straight through with the water.

00:05:31.441 --> 00:05:33.194
Filtering out the dye is the slip to

00:05:33.235 --> 00:05:33.537
avoid.

00:05:35.233 --> 00:05:35.622
Last one.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:38.878
Two liquids, P and Q, mix completely.

00:05:39.501 --> 00:05:42.388
Pure P boils at sixty eight degrees, and

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.635
pure Q boils at seventy two.

00:05:45.458 --> 00:05:47.924
Their first vapour is only slightly richer in

00:05:47.985 --> 00:05:48.927
P than the liquid.

00:05:49.609 --> 00:05:52.531
Explain why simple distillation would separate them poorly,

00:05:52.892 --> 00:05:55.594
and how a fractionating column improves the separation.

00:05:56.376 --> 00:05:57.323
Pause here to answer.

00:06:08.028 --> 00:06:10.473
The boiling points are only four degrees apart,

00:06:10.855 --> 00:06:14.002
so both liquids evaporate together, and one cycle

00:06:14.043 --> 00:06:16.509
gives vapour only a little richer in P.

00:06:17.392 --> 00:06:19.980
A column gives many cycles of condensing and

00:06:20.021 --> 00:06:22.710
boiling again, each one enriching the vapour in

00:06:22.771 --> 00:06:23.011
P.

00:06:23.975 --> 00:06:27.285
And no, the boiling points alone can't guarantee

00:06:27.386 --> 00:06:29.893
pure P, because the mixture might form an

00:06:30.055 --> 00:06:30.616
azeotrope.

00:06:31.259 --> 00:06:33.687
Saying P boils off first at sixty eight,

00:06:33.769 --> 00:06:36.076
leaving pure Q, is the slip to avoid.

00:06:37.922 --> 00:06:41.160
Distillation separates by how easily things evaporate.

00:06:42.045 --> 00:06:44.039
But what about a single drop of black

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:44.361
ink?

00:06:45.046 --> 00:06:46.837
It's a mixture of several dyes, and there's

00:06:46.877 --> 00:06:47.863
only a tiny amount.

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:49.952
Next time, we'll put it on a strip

00:06:49.992 --> 00:06:52.041
of paper, and find out why its dyes

00:06:52.141 --> 00:06:53.406
travel different distances.

00:06:56.295 --> 00:06:57.117
Here are the key notes.

00:06:57.641 --> 00:06:59.188
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:59.229 --> 00:06:59.650
to copy them.
